::meta{
  id: cli.serve
}

`sisho serve <FILE>`: show a document as HTML over HTTP. It is for writing: edit, reload, look at the result.

Status: implemented. It was `tomet serve` and moved here when `tomet` was kept to what a document alone decides.

The page is `tomet html`'s, which `sisho convert --to html` runs too, so what you see is what those write. `tomet` has to be on `PATH`: every request runs it.

=[ Behaviour ]

- Listens on `127.0.0.1` at `--port` (default 8787; `0` takes a free port). Nothing outside the machine can reach it. When it is ready it prints `Serving at http://127.0.0.1:<port> (Ctrl+C to stop)`.
- Every request for `/` runs `tomet html` on `FILE` and returns what it wrote. Nothing is remembered: edit the file and reload the page. The vault's config and the `${...}` evaluation are redone each time.
- `--advanced` and `--lang` mean what they mean for `convert --to html`. There is no `--body`.
- A document that cannot be rendered is answered with an error page (the heading `Could not render`, the file name, and what `tomet` said on its standard error) and HTTP status 500, so the browser says what is wrong. Fix the document and the next reload is back to normal. So is a `tomet` that is not installed: the page says `tomet is not on PATH`.
- Stop it with `Ctrl+C`.

There is no path other than `/`: it shows one document. If the port is already in use, startup fails and the exit code is 1.

=[ Where it differs from `tomet serve` ]

- A document that cannot be rendered is status 500, not 200, and the heading is `Could not render`, not `Parse error` (the cause is not always a parse error).
- `tomet` runs on a thread meant for blocking work, so a slow document does not hold up the server's other work.
- `--port 0` takes a free port, which the ready line then names.

=[ Not decided ]

- Whether to notice a change in the document and reload the page by itself. Today the reload is by hand.
- Whether to show a directory, or the documents a document links to.
